  "subtitle": "[ASSET: left | Teletype | width=24%] This month's VMA goes to <i>Strange Tales</i> 115 ([LINK:1963:ST_115_torch1|ST #115torch|2027-01-27]), where Reed indicates to Johnny that the report of the Sandman's escape from prison came in via \"teletype.\"&nbsp; <i>Heh</i>, the technology quickly went out of fashion in the early 1980s, when personal computers became more prominent and began to phase out these very \"loud, slow, paper-wasting electromechanical machines.\"&nbsp; It's a charming reminder of how quickly technology dates even the most modern‑minded titles.\n[BR]\n[BR] If interested in reading more about Teletype Machines (or even <i>hearing</i> one), click here: [CITATION: https://www.eecis.udel.edu/~davis/cpeg222/AssemblyTutorial/Chapter-05/ass05_6.html].\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Click on the picture of the machine to hear the sound, which I confess, brings back memories...)]\n\n[ASSET: right | FatJuicyPaychecks | width=15%] But I would be remiss to ignore <i>another</i> vintage moment in this month's [LINK:1963:ASM_7|ASM #7|2027-01-21], which shows the Vulture planning to rob the <i>Daily Bugle</i> of its \"nice, fat, juicy <i>pay envelopes</i>,\" well before the days of direct deposit, which rendered the use of actual cash envelopes obsolete.\n[BR]\n[BR] While this was <i>also</i> quite funny now in retrospect, I still give the nod to the Fantastic Four's Teletype Machine!\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Hahahaha!]",
  "review_text": "This month delivered nine graded stories across eight books (<i>Strange Tales </i>once again had two), with a final weighted average of [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold; text=4.35], which was nearly a half point <i>lower</i> than last month's <b>4.79</b>.\n\nThis was particularly surprising given how many titles delivered competent or better material.&nbsp; The Spider-Man offering was <i>quite</i> solid, with Thor and Giant-Man/Wasp both punching in at mid-grade.&nbsp; Even the origin of Dr. Strange only <i>just missed</i> that mark, and the Fantastic Four and Human Torch stories weren't bad either (though hardly worth bragging about).\n\nBut despite all but two offerings being no worse than a readable 4.0, those two bottom-feeders happened to be the full-sized <i>Avengers</i> and <i>X-Men</i>, both of which contributed second issues this month.&nbsp; Suffice it to say that <i>neither</i> second issue justified its existence, <i>especially</i> the <i>Avengers</i>, which seemed to be making a concerted effort to qualify for the 1963 [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Trash Heap of the Year] award.\n\nA shame that a month which offered three stories mid‑grade or better was dragged down by these two poor showings, but... [SPAN: severity=info; text=there you have it.]\n\nOn the positive side, I was pleased by the [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; text=<b>TO BE CONTINUED</b>] ending in <i>Tales to Astonish</i>.&nbsp; It wasn't a \"cliffhanger\" in the sense that someone is depicted about to fall to their doom or an explosion apparently kills our hero -- in this case, the villain was actually <i>far away</i> from the heroes -- but it was encouraging to see that the \"training wheels\" are coming off, and Lee and his various collaborators are beginning to trust the readership enough to sustain a multi‑issue thread.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=A very encouraging sign!]\n\nAnd note that the letters page of [LINK:1963:FF_21|FF #21|2027-01-24] even asks readers if they'd like to \"see more two-part tales like this one!\" (referring to [LINK:1963:TTA_50_giant0|TTA #50|2027-01-15])&nbsp; It's notable to see two books nudging readers toward longer-form storytelling in the same month.\n\nI don't know about <i>them</i>, but [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=I sure would], and seeing as how [PAGE: scenario] has trapped me in 1963 and blocks me from returning to the 21<sup>st</sup> century, all I can promise is that [SPAN: severity=success; text=I'll be back again for more next month!]\n\nI'm ready to dive in <i>now</i>, in fact, but other matters such as staying married, eating, sleeping, exercising, and playing Bridge all conspire to ensure I can't spend every waking hour doing <i>this!</i>\n\n[DEVNOTE: *** SUMMARY_BEGIN ***\n*** THIS MONTH ***9 graded stories over 8 issues...TOTAL WEIGHT: 6.58 grade character issue   weight 7.0   asm       ASM #7  1.00 6.0   thor      JIM #98 0.50 6.0   giant     TTA #50 0.50 5.5   strange   ST #115 0.33 5.0   torch     ST #115 0.50 4.0   ff        FF #21  1.00 4.0   iron      TOS #48 0.75 2.5   xmen      XMEN #2 1.00 1.8   aven      AVEN #2 1.004.35*** LAST MONTH ***7 graded stories over 6 issues...TOTAL WEIGHT: 4.75 grade character issue   weight 6.0   asm       ASM #6  1.00 6.0   ff        FF #20  1.00 6.0   iron      TOS #47 0.75 5.0   torch     ST #114 0.75 2.5   giant     TTA #49 0.50 2.0   strange   ST #114 0.25 1.5   thor      JIM #97 0.504.79\n*** SUMMARY_END ***]",
  "letters_page": "[HANG0] * Introduction of the Space Phantom, Cobra, Human Top, Vanisher, and Hate-Monger\n[HANG0] * Hulk quits the Avengers after only two issues\n[HANG0] * [LINK:1963:JIM_98_thor0|JIM #98|2027-01-12]'s last panel on page 9, complete with a battle cry on the part of Thor - [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=italic; text=well done] -- and absolutely the best panel of the month!\n[HANG0] * First use of cliffhanger ending in [LINK:1963:TTA_50_giant0|TTA #50|2027-01-15]\n[HANG0] * Guest appearance by Nick Fury in the FF (in the <i>present</i> no less, an early crossover that adds welcome texture)\n[HANG0] * New Iron Man armor transitions him closer to his longtime (and more familiar) look\n\n[DEVNOTE: *** INTRODUCTION_BEGIN ***\nSpace Phantom | villain | Milestone: (first)\nCobra | villain | Milestone: (first), (origin)\nWhirlwind | villain | Milestone: (first: as Human Top)\nVanisher | villain | Milestone: (first)\nNotes | first | Fred Duncan\nHate-Monger | villain | Milestone: (first)\nNick Fury | Milestone: (identity: first time as \"Colonel Fury\")\nNotes | first | villain | Mr. Doll (Nathan Dolly)\nNotes | first | Mark II Armor (Red and Gold)\n*** INTRODUCTION_END ***]\n\n<b>Roundup:</b>\n[DEVNOTE: *** HIGHLIGHTS_BEGIN ***]\n[HANG0] * The Avengers stumble through a Space Phantom fiasco so messy you can't help rubbernecking the wreck.\n[HANG0] * A slithering foe and a returning nurse can't distract Thor from rediscovering the joy of battle.\n[HANG0] * The Human Top stays a step ahead of Giant‑Man and the Wasp, right up to a <i>dynamite</i> cliffhanger ending.\n[HANG0] * The X-Men corral the Vanisher after a Danger Room session that lasts longer than the plot.\n[HANG0] * A sharper‑than‑expected Vulture return, complete with Bugle mayhem and Jameson getting exactly what he deserves.\n[HANG0] * The FF melt down under the Hate‑Monger's ray, but Nick Fury shows up and saves the story.\n[HANG0] * Human Torch versus the Sandman -- a fiery detour into Spider‑Man's corner of the universe.\n[HANG0] * Dr. Strange's origin finally arrives, complete with the Ancient One's quiet smirks and Mordo's not‑so‑quiet treachery.\n[HANG0] * Iron Man faces Mr. Doll in a flat tale saved only by the debut of his new red‑and‑gold armor.\n[HANG0] * [SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; text=But <i>most importantly</i>, Jeff reviewed his <b>100<sup>th</sup></b> issue this month!]\n[DEVNOTE: *** HIGHLIGHTS_END ***]",
  "ownership": "[HANG0] * Thor has yet <i>another</i> new menace to deal with\n[HANG0] * Hank and Jan take the next step toward trying to stop their whirling enemy\n[HANG0] * New powers for the Invisible Girl\n[HANG0] * Ben and Johnny battle each <i>other</i>\n[HANG0] * Iron Man meets X-Men's Angel\n[HANG0] * And what's <i>this</i>?&nbsp; Peter Parker boxing with Flash Thompson?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Whoa!]"
}